Does anyone actually compile nethack without any of the 'fun stuff' in
config.h? Ie sinks, wallified mazes, the rogue level, kops, foocubus
seduction, steeds, tourists and elbereth? These things can all
optionally be #ifdef'ed out of the source, but does anyone actually do
so?

The big problem with starting to introduce #ifdefs is that they linger,
and they multiply. Tourists have been a part of the game (and a part of
the whole "nethack mythos") for many versions, but still they are an
'optional' piece of code.

As a patch writer, maintaining all the #ifdef'ed code is a pain in the
bum. If I want to add a new kind of tool, for example, I have to add
special cases to objects.c to make sure the probabilities balance
appropriately whether or not TOURIST and SADDLE are defined.

There are 192 snippets of code which have "#ifdef STEED" wrapped around
them. Many of these are just a single "!u.usteed". They make the code a
lot harder to follow. Do we really need them?

